b/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527904 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527904 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527904 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527904 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527904 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527904 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527904 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186527660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527904 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f913ee15-c6e1-5a0b-bf56-708c893775f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527915 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527915 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94780" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527915 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527915 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the elliptic cone ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2{z}^{2}$ and the line $x=y=z+6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527915 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0c6c3541-8949-51d1-86fa-598cbd047050" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527915 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\nx=y=z+6=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t \\\\ y=t \\\\ z=t-6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2{z}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+{t}^{2} & =2(t-6)^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+{t}^{2} & =2({t}^{2}-12t+36) \\\\ 2{t}^{2} & =2{t}^{2}-24t+72 \\\\ 24t & =72 \\\\ t & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solution back into the equations of the line. For $t=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=3 \\\\ y=3 \\\\ z=3-6=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(3,3-3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527938 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527938 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94952" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527938 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527938 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following best describes the projection of the intersection between the elliptic cone ${x}^{2}+2(y-1)^{2}=6(z+3)^{2}$ and the plane $x+y-4=0$ onto the $yz$-plane?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527938 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4b5ee114-4f6d-532e-ba84-9309871f16a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527938 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx+y-4=0\\,⟹\\,x=4-y\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4-y)^{2}+2(y-1)^{2} & =6(z+3)^{2} \\\\ 16-8y+{y}^{2}+2({y}^{2}-2y+1) & =6(z+3)^{2} \\\\ 16-8y+{y}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-4y+2 & =6(z+3)^{2} \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-12y+18 & =6(z+3)^{2} \\\\ 3({y}^{2}-4y+4)-12+18 & =6(z+3)^{2} \\\\ 3(y-2)^{2}+6 & =6(z+3)^{2} \\\\ 6(z+3)^{2}-3(y-2)^{2} & =6 \\\\ (z+3)^{2}-\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of a hyperbola centered at $(2,-3)$ in the $yz$-plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527945 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527945 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94084" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527945 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527945 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the elliptic cone ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=8{z}^{2}$ and the line $\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{2}=z-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527945 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5a9b6c0a-2d84-570a-8860-6a6e61ceeed3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527945 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{2}=z-1=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4t \\\\ y=2t \\\\ z=t+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2} & =8{z}^{2} \\\\ (4t)^{2}+4(2t)^{2} & =8(t+1)^{2} \\\\ 16{t}^{2}+16{t}^{2} & =8(t+1)^{2} \\\\ 32{t}^{2} & =8(t+1)^{2} \\\\ 4{t}^{2} & =(t+1)^{2} \\\\ 4{t}^{2} & ={t}^{2}+2t+1 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-2t-1 & =0 \\\\ (3t+1)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{3},1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4(-\\frac{1}{3})=-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ y=2(-\\frac{1}{3})=-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ z=-\\frac{1}{3}+1=\\frac{2}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(-\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3}).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4(1)=4 \\\\ y=2(1)=2 \\\\ z=1+1=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(4,2,2).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$ and $(4,2,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527951 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527951 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73867" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527951 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527951 #edb/ref 1071 "For the elliptic paraboloid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{3}=z+4$, the section in the plane $\\sqrt{3}x-2y=4$ is a parabola. \nFind the vertex of the projection of this parabola onto $yz$-plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527951 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "65227920-db32-5f4f-9774-747e0bc8ffd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527951 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}x-2y=4\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2(y+2)}{\\sqrt{3}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{(\\frac{2(y+2)}{\\sqrt{3}})}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{3} & =z+4 \\\\ \\frac{4(y+2)^{2}}{3\\cdot 2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{3} & =z+4 \\\\ \\frac{2(y+2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{3} & =z+4 \\\\ \\frac{3(y+2)^{2}}{3} & =z+4 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =z+4 \\\\ z & =(y+2)^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the vertex of the projection onto $yz$-plane is $(-2,-4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527981 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527981 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94951" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527981 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527981 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following best describes the projection of the intersection between the elliptic cone $(x-2)^{2}+2(y+1)^{2}=2{z}^{2}$ and the plane $x+2z-3=0$ onto the $yz$-plane?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527981 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b35b0faf-35a9-5d63-9b1f-dccf9512ee2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527981 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx+2z-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3-2z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-2z-2)^{2}+2(y+1)^{2} & =2{z}^{2} \\\\ (1-2z)^{2}+2(y+1)^{2} & =2{z}^{2} \\\\ 1-4z+4{z}^{2}+2(y+1)^{2} & =2{z}^{2} \\\\ 2{z}^{2}-4z+1+2(y+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2(z-1)^{2}-2+1+2(y+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2(z-1)^{2}+2(y+1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y+1)^{2}+(z-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of a circle of radius $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ centered at $(-1,1)$ in the $yz$-plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
